insert into kairo.questions (id, subject, topic, subtopic, learning_objective, concepts_tested, difficulty_rating, cognitive_level, estimated_solving_time_sec, reading_load, calculation_load, distractors, source, year, exam_body, stem, options, correct_option, explanation, lifecycle_state, distractor_rationale) values
($q$chemistry_0501$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Structural isomerism of alkanes$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Structural isomerism of alkanes.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "j32bof", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This sounds like a plausible structural name, but it doesn't correspond to a legitimate, separately-named isomer under IUPAC rules.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Butane is a 4-carbon chain (C4H10) - a different molecular formula entirely, not a match for C5H12.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "2-methylpropane (isobutane) is also C4H10, one carbon short of the C5H12 formula being asked about.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$An isomer of C5H12 is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "2-ethylbutane", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "butane", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2-methylbutane", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "2-methylpropane", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Isomers of C5H12 must share that exact molecular formula while having a different structure. Checking each option's real formula settles it fast: butane and 2-methylpropane are both C4H10 - one carbon short, so neither can match C5H12 at all. '2-ethylbutane' isn't even a genuine distinct compound name (extending butane with an ethyl branch on carbon 2 just gives back a longer chain, correctly renamed 3-methylpentane). That leaves 2-methylbutane, which genuinely has the formula C5H12 and is one of pentane's real structural isomers.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This sounds like a plausible structural name, but it doesn't correspond to a legitimate, separately-named isomer under IUPAC rules. B: Butane is a 4-carbon chain (C4H10) - a different molecular formula entirely, not a match for C5H12. D: 2-methylpropane (isobutane) is also C4H10, one carbon short of the C5H12 formula being asked about.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0502$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Esterification and hydrolysis$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Esterification and hydrolysis.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "kmmznm", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Saponification is a specific type of ester hydrolysis carried out with a strong base like NaOH (producing soap and glycerol) - it's a special case, not the general reverse reaction shown here.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Fermentation describes microbial conversion of sugars into ethanol and CO2 - it has nothing to do with breaking down an ester.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Hydration describes water adding across a double bond (like turning an alkene into an alcohol) - a different reaction type from ester breakdown.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Alkanol + Alkanoic acid ⇌ Ester + Water. The reverse reaction of the equation above is known as$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "saponification", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "hydrolysis", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "fermentation", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "hydration", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$Reversing the equation means breaking an ester back apart into its alcohol and acid using water - and 'breaking apart with water' is exactly what hydrolysis means, in the most general sense.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Saponification is a specific type of ester hydrolysis carried out with a strong base like NaOH (producing soap and glycerol) - it's a special case, not the general reverse reaction shown here. C: Fermentation describes microbial conversion of sugars into ethanol and CO2 - it has nothing to do with breaking down an ester. D: Hydration describes water adding across a double bond (like turning an alkene into an alcohol) - a different reaction type from ester breakdown.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0503$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Decarboxylation of carboxylic acids$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Decarboxylation of carboxylic acids.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "dcbxq2", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Acidification describes making something more acidic (adding H+ ions) - it doesn't describe this bond-breaking loss of CO2.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Esterification is an acid reacting with an alcohol to form an ester - a completely different reaction with a different set of reactants.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Carboxylation means ADDING a carboxyl group to a molecule - the exact opposite direction of what's happening in this reaction.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$CH3COOH(g) → CH4(g) + CO2(g). The reaction above is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "acidification", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "esterification", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "decarboxylation", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "carboxylation", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Look at exactly what's leaving the molecule: the carboxylic acid loses its -COOH group entirely as CO2, leaving just CH4 behind. Removing a carboxyl group as carbon dioxide is precisely the definition of decarboxylation.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Acidification describes making something more acidic (adding H+ ions) - it doesn't describe this bond-breaking loss of CO2. B: Esterification is an acid reacting with an alcohol to form an ester - a completely different reaction with a different set of reactants. D: Carboxylation means ADDING a carboxyl group to a molecule - the exact opposite direction of what's happening in this reaction.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0504$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Organic Chemistry$q$, $q$Reactivity of saturated hydrocarbons$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Reactivity of saturated hydrocarbons.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "r4t0dz", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Neutralization is an acid-base reaction type; alkanes aren't acids or bases, so this doesn't describe them at all.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Addition reactions need a double or triple bond to add across - that's the hallmark of alkenes and alkynes, not saturated alkanes.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Elimination reactions remove atoms to CREATE a double bond (moving away from an alkane-like structure toward an alkene) - the reverse of what characterizes alkane behaviour itself.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A characteristic of the alkane family is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "substitution reaction", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "neutralization reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "addition reaction", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "elimination reaction", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Alkanes are fully saturated - every carbon already has the maximum number of single bonds it can hold, so there's no double or triple bond available to react across. Instead, alkanes react by having one of their hydrogens swapped out for another atom, like in free-radical halogenation - a substitution reaction.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Neutralization is an acid-base reaction type; alkanes aren't acids or bases, so this doesn't describe them at all. C: Addition reactions need a double or triple bond to add across - that's the hallmark of alkenes and alkynes, not saturated alkanes. D: Elimination reactions remove atoms to CREATE a double bond (moving away from an alkane-like structure toward an alkene) - the reverse of what characterizes alkane behaviour itself.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0505$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Environmental Chemistry$q$, $q$Soil chemistry and metal ion mobility$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Soil chemistry and metal ion mobility.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "u8nzau", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is backwards - alkaline (basic) conditions tend to precipitate many metal ions as insoluble hydroxides, actually reducing their mobility rather than increasing it.", "misconceptionId": "sign_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "High nitrate levels are a pollution concern in their own right, but nitrates don't have the same strong effect on metal ion solubility that soil acidity does.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Chloride content doesn't drive metal ions out of soil minerals the way acidity does.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Pollution of underground water by metal ions is very likely in a soil that has high$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "alkalinity", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "nitrate content", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "acidity", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "chloride content", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Metal ions can only leach into groundwater once they're released as free, dissolved ions rather than locked up in insoluble soil minerals. Acidic soil (high H+ concentration) actively dissolves metal-bearing minerals and displaces metal ions from soil particles, freeing them up to migrate downward into the water table.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is backwards - alkaline (basic) conditions tend to precipitate many metal ions as insoluble hydroxides, actually reducing their mobility rather than increasing it. B: High nitrate levels are a pollution concern in their own right, but nitrates don't have the same strong effect on metal ion solubility that soil acidity does. D: Chloride content doesn't drive metal ions out of soil minerals the way acidity does.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0506$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Solutions$q$, $q$Solubility calculations (mol dm-3)$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Solubility calculations (mol dm-3).$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "9jaxsa", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This is roughly double the correct moles figure before scaling, consistent with a slip somewhere in working out the molar mass.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This is close to the unscaled moles-per-100g figure (0.125) but stops there - it's missing the final x10 step needed to convert to a per-dm3 value.", "misconceptionId": "unit_conversion_error"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This overshoots the correct scaled value, likely from an error earlier in the molar mass or mole calculation that then got multiplied through.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The solubility in mol dm-3 of 20g of CuSO4 dissolved in 100g of water at 18°C is [Cu = 64, S = 32, O = 16]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "0.25", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "0.13", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2.00", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "1.25", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Work through this in two stages. First, find the moles dissolved: CuSO4's molar mass = 64 + 32 + (16x4) = 160 g/mol, so 20g is 20 / 160 = 0.125 mol - and that's per 100g (roughly 100 cm3) of water. To express solubility per dm3 (1000 cm3), scale that figure up by 10: 0.125 x 10 = 1.25 mol dm-3.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This is roughly double the correct moles figure before scaling, consistent with a slip somewhere in working out the molar mass. B: This is close to the unscaled moles-per-100g figure (0.125) but stops there - it's missing the final x10 step needed to convert to a per-dm3 value. C: This overshoots the correct scaled value, likely from an error earlier in the molar mass or mole calculation that then got multiplied through.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0507$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Acid-Base Chemistry$q$, $q$Normal salts vs. acid salts$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Normal salts vs. acid salts.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "6y8jif", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "The leftover 'H' in the formula (sodium hydrogen carbonate) shows one acidic hydrogen wasn't replaced - this makes it an acid salt, not a normal salt.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Same pattern here - sodium hydrogen sulphate still carries one unreplaced acidic hydrogen, classifying it as an acid salt.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "NaHS also has a leftover replaceable hydrogen, making it an acid salt rather than a normal one.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of these compounds is a normal salt?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Na2CO3", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "NaHCO3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "NaHSO4", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "NaHS", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$A normal salt forms when ALL the replaceable (acidic) hydrogens of the parent acid have been swapped out for a metal ion, leaving none behind. Na2CO3 comes from carbonic acid with both of its acidic hydrogens replaced by sodium. Look closely at the other three options - each one still has a lone 'H' sitting in the formula, meaning one acidic hydrogen wasn't replaced, which makes them acid salts instead.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: The leftover 'H' in the formula (sodium hydrogen carbonate) shows one acidic hydrogen wasn't replaced - this makes it an acid salt, not a normal salt. C: Same pattern here - sodium hydrogen sulphate still carries one unreplaced acidic hydrogen, classifying it as an acid salt. D: NaHS also has a leftover replaceable hydrogen, making it an acid salt rather than a normal one.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0508$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Environmental and Health Chemistry$q$, $q$Carcinogenic substances$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Carcinogenic substances.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "4w8vof", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Nitrogen(II) oxide is toxic and contributes to smog and acid rain, but it isn't classified as a carcinogen the way asbestos is.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Carbon(II) oxide is dangerously toxic by a different mechanism entirely - it binds to haemoglobin and causes asphyxiation, rather than causing cancer.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Sawdust can act as a general respiratory irritant in some settings, but it doesn't carry the well-established cancer risk that asbestos does.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A carcinogenic substance is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "nitrogen(II) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "carbon(II) oxide", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "asbestos dust", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "sawdust", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$This is a straight recall point worth locking in: asbestos fibers are a well-documented carcinogen, and prolonged inhalation is linked to lung diseases including mesothelioma and lung cancer.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Nitrogen(II) oxide is toxic and contributes to smog and acid rain, but it isn't classified as a carcinogen the way asbestos is. B: Carbon(II) oxide is dangerously toxic by a different mechanism entirely - it binds to haemoglobin and causes asphyxiation, rather than causing cancer. D: Sawdust can act as a general respiratory irritant in some settings, but it doesn't carry the well-established cancer risk that asbestos does.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0509$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Quantitative Chemistry$q$, $q$Acid-base neutralization volume calculations$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Acid-base neutralization volume calculations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "e6pk77", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This value shows up if the 1:2 mole ratio between H2SO4 and NaOH is skipped, treating the reaction as a simple 1:1 acid-base match instead.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This doesn't correspond to a clean, correct setup of the stoichiometry - it suggests the given quantities were combined in the wrong order somewhere in the working.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This also doesn't map to a correct substitution of the numbers - likely from dividing the wrong pair of quantities against each other.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$What volume of 0.5 mol dm-3 H2SO4 will exactly neutralize 20 cm3 of 0.1 mol dm-3 NaOH solution?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "5.0cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "6.8cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "8.3cm3", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "2.0cm3", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Start with the balanced equation: H2SO4 + 2NaOH → Na2SO4 + 2H2O - notice 1 mole of acid neutralizes 2 moles of base, not a 1:1 match. Moles of NaOH = 0.020 dm3 x 0.1 mol dm-3 = 0.002 mol. Moles of H2SO4 needed = 0.002 / 2 = 0.001 mol. Volume of H2SO4 = moles / concentration = 0.001 / 0.5 = 0.002 dm3 = 2.0 cm3.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This value shows up if the 1:2 mole ratio between H2SO4 and NaOH is skipped, treating the reaction as a simple 1:1 acid-base match instead. B: This doesn't correspond to a clean, correct setup of the stoichiometry - it suggests the given quantities were combined in the wrong order somewhere in the working. C: This also doesn't map to a correct substitution of the numbers - likely from dividing the wrong pair of quantities against each other.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0510$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Solutions$q$, $q$Sparingly soluble salts$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Sparingly soluble salts.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "mu3oy8", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$application$q$, 37, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "A colloid involves particles that are dispersed but not truly dissolved, sitting between a true solution and a suspension in size - that's not what's happening with the small dissolved fraction of CaSO4.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Suspended particles are visibly undissolved solid that will eventually settle - the CaSO4 that has actually gone into solution is truly dissolved, not floating as suspended solid.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "A precipitate is an insoluble solid that forms and separates out - that describes the undissolved excess CaSO4 sitting at the bottom, not the sparingly dissolved portion the question is asking about.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Calcium tetraoxosulphate(VI) dissolves in water only sparingly to form a$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "colloid", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "solution", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "suspension", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "precipitate", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$'Sparingly soluble' just means a small, limited amount dissolves - it doesn't mean nothing dissolves at all. Whatever CaSO4 does go into solution is genuinely dissolved at the ion level, forming a true solution (just a dilute or weak one), not some in-between state like a colloid or suspension.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: A colloid involves particles that are dispersed but not truly dissolved, sitting between a true solution and a suspension in size - that's not what's happening with the small dissolved fraction of CaSO4. C: Suspended particles are visibly undissolved solid that will eventually settle - the CaSO4 that has actually gone into solution is truly dissolved, not floating as suspended solid. D: A precipitate is an insoluble solid that forms and separates out - that describes the undissolved excess CaSO4 sitting at the bottom, not the sparingly dissolved portion the question is asking about.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0511$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Water Chemistry$q$, $q$Hardness of water$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Hardness of water.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "beypy2", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "Calcium is half right, but sodium salts are generally soluble and don't precipitate with soap - pairing calcium with the wrong partner ion is a common mix-up.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Magnesium is correctly one of the hardness ions, but silver isn't naturally present in ordinary water supplies in amounts that matter - this option swaps in an unrelated metal.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "These are both Group 1 metals whose compounds are freely soluble in water - memorizing 'metal ions cause hardness' without knowing which ones leads students here.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Hardness of water is caused by the presence of the ions of$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "calcium and magnesium", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "calcium and sodium", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "magnesium and silver", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "sodium and potassium", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$This is testing whether you know what actually causes water hardness. Hard water contains dissolved calcium (Ca2+) and magnesium (Mg2+) ions, usually picked up as water passes through limestone or chalk deposits. These ions react with soap to form an insoluble scum instead of lather, and they're the ones responsible for the 'furring' you see in kettles. Sodium and potassium salts, by contrast, are almost always soluble and don't cause this problem - that's your quick test for eliminating options B and D.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: Calcium is half right, but sodium salts are generally soluble and don't precipitate with soap - pairing calcium with the wrong partner ion is a common mix-up. C: Magnesium is correctly one of the hardness ions, but silver isn't naturally present in ordinary water supplies in amounts that matter - this option swaps in an unrelated metal. D: These are both Group 1 metals whose compounds are freely soluble in water - memorizing 'metal ions cause hardness' without knowing which ones leads students here.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0512$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$States of Matter$q$, $q$Kinetic theory of gases$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Kinetic theory of gases.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "unbitv", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Collisions do happen constantly in a gas, but that's a consequence of random motion, not the reason the motion is random in the first place - this option describes an effect, not the cause.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Molecule size isn't the deciding factor - solids are built from equally small particles yet still pack into an orderly lattice. Size alone doesn't explain disorder.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "'No definite shape' is actually describing the same disorder the question is asking you to explain - it's restating the observation, not giving the underlying reason for it.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$It is difficult to achieve an orderly arrangement of the molecules of a gas because they$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "can collide with one another in the container", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "are too small in size", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "have little force of attraction between them", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "have no definite shape", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Think about what makes solids orderly in the first place - strong intermolecular forces lock particles into fixed positions. Gas molecules have almost negligible forces of attraction between them, so nothing holds them in place; they fly around randomly and can't settle into a pattern. That weak attraction is the root cause - everything else you observe about gases (no fixed shape, constant collisions) flows from it.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Collisions do happen constantly in a gas, but that's a consequence of random motion, not the reason the motion is random in the first place - this option describes an effect, not the cause. B: Molecule size isn't the deciding factor - solids are built from equally small particles yet still pack into an orderly lattice. Size alone doesn't explain disorder. D: 'No definite shape' is actually describing the same disorder the question is asking you to explain - it's restating the observation, not giving the underlying reason for it.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0513$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure$q$, $q$Shapes of atomic orbitals$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Shapes of atomic orbitals.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "i5jfdn", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 1, $q$recall$q$, 25, $q$low$q$, $q$none$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This borrows from the older Bohr-Sommerfeld model of elliptical electron orbits, which described paths, not the quantum-mechanical orbital shapes JAMB is testing here.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "There's no real orbital shape described as 'spiral' in atomic theory - this looks like a guess rather than a recalled fact.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This mixes up the 2-D circular orbit picture from the old Bohr model with the actual 3-D orbital shape - orbitals are regions of probability in three dimensions, not flat circular paths.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The shape of the s-orbital is$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "elliptical", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "spiral", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "circular", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "spherical", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$This is straight recall from atomic structure: the s-orbital is spherical, meaning the probability of finding the electron is the same in every direction from the nucleus. That's what makes it different from p-orbitals, which are dumbbell-shaped and directional.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This borrows from the older Bohr-Sommerfeld model of elliptical electron orbits, which described paths, not the quantum-mechanical orbital shapes JAMB is testing here. B: There's no real orbital shape described as 'spiral' in atomic theory - this looks like a guess rather than a recalled fact. C: This mixes up the 2-D circular orbit picture from the old Bohr model with the actual 3-D orbital shape - orbitals are regions of probability in three dimensions, not flat circular paths.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0514$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Reactions$q$, $q$Flammability and combustion of gases$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Flammability and combustion of gases.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "vejcxc", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Both helium and neon are noble gases with completely filled outer shells, so neither reacts or burns - picking this suggests forgetting that inertness is the defining feature of the noble gases.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Neon is inert, and nitrogen - despite being reactive in some contexts like the Haber process - doesn't burn as a fuel under normal conditions; neither of these two ignites in flame.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Nitrogen and helium: one is a noble gas, the other is largely unreactive under ordinary conditions - no fuel gas is present in this pair, so there's nothing to burn.", "misconceptionId": "memorized_not_understood"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Which of the following mixtures of gases is likely to burn in flame?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "Helium and neon", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "Neon and nitrogen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "Neon and hydrogen", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "D", "text": "Nitrogen and helium", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$C$q$, $q$Scan each pair for a gas that's actually combustible. Helium and neon are both noble gases - completely unreactive, they won't burn under any normal condition. Nitrogen doesn't support or undergo combustion easily either. Hydrogen is the odd one out here: it's highly flammable and burns readily in air. Since hydrogen appears only in option C, that pairing is the one that will burn - neon just tags along inertly.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Both helium and neon are noble gases with completely filled outer shells, so neither reacts or burns - picking this suggests forgetting that inertness is the defining feature of the noble gases. B: Neon is inert, and nitrogen - despite being reactive in some contexts like the Haber process - doesn't burn as a fuel under normal conditions; neither of these two ignites in flame. D: Nitrogen and helium: one is a noble gas, the other is largely unreactive under ordinary conditions - no fuel gas is present in this pair, so there's nothing to burn.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0515$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Chemical Bonding$q$, $q$Electronegativity and bond polarity$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Electronegativity and bond polarity.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "3a2yze", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This is the opposite property - electropositivity describes how easily an atom loses electrons to form positive ions, which is the behavior of metals, not chlorine.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Electron affinity measures the energy released when a gaseous atom gains an electron - it's related but it's not the property that directly explains why a bond becomes polar; that's electronegativity.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "Electrovalency describes the combining capacity of an ion in ionic bonding - it's a description of the bond type itself, not the underlying atomic property that causes the ionic character.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$The property of chlorine which causes hydrogen chloride to be more ionic than the chlorine molecule is its$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "electronegativity", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "electropositivity", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "electron affinity", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "electrovalency", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$In Cl2, both atoms pull electrons equally hard, so the bond is purely covalent with no charge separation. In HCl, chlorine is far more electronegative than hydrogen, so it pulls the shared electron pair closer to itself, creating a partial negative charge on Cl and partial positive on H. That unequal pull - electronegativity - is exactly what gives HCl its extra ionic character compared to Cl2.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This is the opposite property - electropositivity describes how easily an atom loses electrons to form positive ions, which is the behavior of metals, not chlorine. C: Electron affinity measures the energy released when a gaseous atom gains an electron - it's related but it's not the property that directly explains why a bond becomes polar; that's electronegativity. D: Electrovalency describes the combining capacity of an ion in ionic bonding - it's a description of the bond type itself, not the underlying atomic property that causes the ionic character.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0516$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Air and Combustion$q$, $q$Composition of air after rusting experiment$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Composition of air after rusting experiment.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "txr2h3", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 3, $q$application$q$, 57, $q$medium$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Oxygen is actually the gas that's been removed from X during the experiment - it's what got consumed by the rusting process, which is why the water level rises. Picking this misses the whole point of the demonstration.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Water is the sealing liquid used to trap the air and show the volume change - it isn't a component of the gas mixture X itself.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "'Impurities' is too vague for a chemistry answer - the specific, correct term for the unreactive gases present in air (argon and friends) is 'inert gas'.", "misconceptionId": "misunderstood_terminology"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In the experiment above, X is a mixture of nitrogen, carbon(IV) oxide and$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "oxygen", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "inert gas", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "C", "text": "water", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "impurities", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$B$q$, $q$This question comes from the classic 'rusting nail in trapped air' set-up, where a tube of air is inverted over water with an iron nail inside. As the iron rusts, it consumes the oxygen from the trapped air, and water rises to take up the volume oxygen used to occupy. What's left in the tube (X) is no longer oxygen - it's been used up - so X is really nitrogen (the bulk of air), a small residual amount of carbon(IV) oxide, and the inert/noble gases (like argon) that don't react with the rusting iron at all.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Oxygen is actually the gas that's been removed from X during the experiment - it's what got consumed by the rusting process, which is why the water level rises. Picking this misses the whole point of the demonstration. C: Water is the sealing liquid used to trap the air and show the volume change - it isn't a component of the gas mixture X itself. D: 'Impurities' is too vague for a chemistry answer - the specific, correct term for the unreactive gases present in air (argon and friends) is 'inert gas'.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0517$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Gaseous State$q$, $q$Graham's Law of diffusion$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Graham's Law of diffusion.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "84csd7", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This suggests an arithmetic slip somewhere in applying the square-root ratio rather than an error in the law itself - always double check your square root and multiplication before locking in an answer.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "Choosing the same time as methane means treating diffusion rate as if molar mass doesn't matter at all - but Graham's Law exists precisely because heavier molecules move slower.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "This comes from inverting the ratio - using sqrt(16/64) instead of sqrt(64/16) - which wrongly implies the heavier gas diffuses faster and takes less time.", "misconceptionId": "formula_recall_error"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$A given volume of methane diffuses in 20s. How long will it take the same volume of sulphur(IV) oxide to diffuse under the same conditions? [C=12, H=1, S=32, O=16]$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "40s", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "60s", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "20s", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "5s", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$This is Graham's Law: heavier gases diffuse more slowly, so they take longer. First, work out the molar masses - methane (CH4) = 12 + (4x1) = 16, and sulphur(IV) oxide (SO2) = 32 + (2x16) = 64. Graham's Law says time is proportional to the square root of molar mass, so t(SO2)/t(CH4) = sqrt(64/16) = sqrt(4) = 2. Since methane took 20s, SO2 - being 4 times heavier - takes twice as long: 20 x 2 = 40s.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This suggests an arithmetic slip somewhere in applying the square-root ratio rather than an error in the law itself - always double check your square root and multiplication before locking in an answer. C: Choosing the same time as methane means treating diffusion rate as if molar mass doesn't matter at all - but Graham's Law exists precisely because heavier molecules move slower. D: This comes from inverting the ratio - using sqrt(16/64) instead of sqrt(64/16) - which wrongly implies the heavier gas diffuses faster and takes less time.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0518$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure$q$, $q$Isotopes and relative abundance$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Isotopes and relative abundance.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "gzi1bc", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 72, $q$low$q$, $q$heavy$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "This doesn't correspond to the 3:1 ratio at all - it looks like a value pulled from a different calculation or a slip while converting the ratio to a percentage.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "Close to the correct answer but not quite - likely from a small rounding or division error when converting the 1-in-4 ratio into a percentage.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is actually the abundance of the mass-35 isotope, not mass-37 - a classic case of doing the calculation correctly but reporting the wrong isotope's share.", "misconceptionId": "confused_similar_concepts"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$Chlorine consists of two isotopes of mass numbers 35 and 37 in the ratio 3:1, and has an average atomic mass of 35.5. Calculate the relative abundance of the isotope of mass number 37.$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "60", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "20", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "75", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "25", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$Don't rush past the ratio - 3:1 means out of every 4 parts, 3 are the lighter isotope (mass 35) and 1 is the heavier one (mass 37). Converting to percentages: Cl-35 makes up 3/4 = 75%, and Cl-37 makes up 1/4 = 25%. You can check this works: (0.75 x 35) + (0.25 x 37) = 26.25 + 9.25 = 35.5, which matches the given atomic mass exactly. The question asks specifically for the abundance of the mass-37 isotope, so the answer is 25%.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: This doesn't correspond to the 3:1 ratio at all - it looks like a value pulled from a different calculation or a slip while converting the ratio to a percentage. B: Close to the correct answer but not quite - likely from a small rounding or division error when converting the 1-in-4 ratio into a percentage. C: This is actually the abundance of the mass-35 isotope, not mass-37 - a classic case of doing the calculation correctly but reporting the wrong isotope's share.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0519$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Periodic Table$q$, $q$Electron configuration of halide ions$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Electron configuration of halide ions.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "7u41s5", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "B", "explanation": "This is the valence electron count of the neutral halogen atom before anything happens - it's easy to stop here if you don't carry through the 'electron added' part of the question.", "misconceptionId": "rushed_under_pressure"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This looks like it's borrowing the duet rule that applies to very light elements like hydrogen and helium, which doesn't apply to halogens at all.", "misconceptionId": "overgeneralized_rule"}, {"option": "D", "explanation": "There's no chemical reasoning that leads here - this doesn't match any step of forming a halide ion from a halogen atom.", "misconceptionId": "guessed_no_reasoning"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$An electron can be added to a halogen atom to form a halide ion with$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "8 valence electrons", "isCorrect": true}, {"label": "B", "text": "7 valence electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "2 valence electrons", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "3 valence electrons", "isCorrect": false}]$q$::jsonb, $q$A$q$, $q$Halogens sit in Group 17, so a neutral halogen atom already has 7 valence electrons. The question specifically says an electron is added - that's what forms the halide ion (like Cl-). Adding one more electron to 7 gives 8, a full and stable octet. That's the whole reason halogens are so eager to gain an electron in the first place - it completes their outer shell.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$B: This is the valence electron count of the neutral halogen atom before anything happens - it's easy to stop here if you don't carry through the 'electron added' part of the question. C: This looks like it's borrowing the duet rule that applies to very light elements like hydrogen and helium, which doesn't apply to halogens at all. D: There's no chemical reasoning that leads here - this doesn't match any step of forming a halide ion from a halogen atom.$q$),
($q$chemistry_0520$q$, $q$Chemistry$q$, $q$Atomic Structure & Bonding$q$, $q$Radioactivity and Half-Life$q$, $q$Answer a real JAMB 2004 question on Alpha decay equations.$q$, $q$[{"conceptId": "ot0voy", "weight": 1}]$q$::jsonb, 2, $q$application$q$, 45, $q$low$q$, $q$light$q$, $q$[{"option": "A", "explanation": "Keeping the mass number unchanged ignores that the alpha particle carries away mass - in any decay, something is always lost, so the daughter nucleus's mass number must be smaller than the parent's.", "misconceptionId": "missing_prerequisite"}, {"option": "B", "explanation": "This subtracts too much from 226 - likely losing track of the fact that an alpha particle only carries away 4 mass units, not 6.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}, {"option": "C", "explanation": "This is an increase rather than a decrease - in alpha decay mass number always goes down, never up, so this direction of change doesn't fit the physics of the reaction.", "misconceptionId": "arithmetic_slip"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$jamb_past_question$q$, 2004, $q$JAMB$q$, $q$In the nuclear equation Ra-226 (atomic number 88) decays to Rn-x (atomic number 86) by emitting an alpha particle, what is the value of x?$q$, $q$[{"label": "A", "text": "226", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "B", "text": "220", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "C", "text": "227", "isCorrect": false}, {"label": "D", "text": "222", "isCorrect": true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$D$q$, $q$In any nuclear equation, mass number and atomic number must balance on both sides. An alpha particle is a helium nucleus (mass number 4, atomic number 2), so when radium emits one, it loses 4 from its mass number and 2 from its atomic number. Starting from radium's mass number of 226: 226 - 4 = 222. Check the atomic number too: 88 - 2 = 86, which matches radon's atomic number given in the equation - so x = 222 is confirmed from both sides balancing.$q$, $q$live$q$, $q$A: Keeping the mass number unchanged ignores that the alpha particle carries away mass - in any decay, something is always lost, so the daughter nucleus's mass number must be smaller than the parent's. B: This subtracts too much from 226 - likely losing track of the fact that an alpha particle only carries away 4 mass units, not 6. C: This is an increase rather than a decrease - in alpha decay mass number always goes down, never up, so this direction of change doesn't fit the physics of the reaction.$q$);
